## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cache invalidation and per-tenant isolation with Redis. It emphasizes correctness over theory and keeps memory footprints predictable.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern with tenant keys
- TTL tuning and avoidance of stampedes
- Pub/Sub vs. central bus for invalidation

## Code Example
```javascript
const KEY = (tid, pid) => `tenant:${tid}:product:${pid}`
async function getWithCache(tid, pid, loadFromDb, cache, ttl=300){
  const k = KEY(tid, pid)
  let v = await cache.get(k)
  if (v) return JSON.parse(v)
  const row = await loadFromDb(tid, pid)
  await cache.set(k, JSON.stringify(row), ttl)
  return row
}
// on update
async function invalidate(tid, pid, pub, cache){
  await pub.publish(`tenant:${tid}:cache:product`, JSON.stringify({pid}))
  await cache.del(KEY(tid,pid))
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle cross-tenant eviction storms if many tenants update simultaneously?
- How would you monitor cache hit rate and TTL effectiveness in production?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests experience with cross-cache coherence, pub/sub-based invalidation, tombstone patterns, and stampede protection in production environments.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-layer cache invalidation with pub/sub synchronization
- Tombstones to prevent cache penetration during outages
- Per-key versioning for monotonic reads
- Handling partial outages and race conditions
- Read-path validation against primary version

## Code Example

```javascript
// Update path
async function updateProduct(id, data) {
  const newVersion = await redis.incr(`product:${id}:ver`);
  await redis.set(`product:${id}`, JSON.stringify({ ...data, version: newVersion }));
  await redis.publish('cache:invalidate', JSON.stringify({ key: `product:${id}`, version: newVersion }));
}

// Invalidation handler (runs on Memcached instances)
async function handleInvalidation(message) {
  const { key, version } = JSON.parse(message);
  await memcached.delete(key);
  await memcached.set(`${key}:tombstone`, version, { ttl: 300 });
}

// Read path with version validation
async function getProduct(id) {
  const [memcachedData, currentVersion] = await Promise.all([
    memcached.get(`product:${id}`),
    redis.get(`product:${id}:ver`)
  ]);

  if (memcachedData) {
    const cachedVersion = JSON.parse(memcachedData).version;
    if (cachedVersion === currentVersion) return JSON.parse(memcachedData);
  }

  const freshData = await redis.get(`product:${id}`);
  await memcached.set(`product:${id}`, freshData);
  return JSON.parse(freshData);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of token binding, rotation, and revocation across regions in a mixed IdP environment. It emphasizes concrete data modeling and fault handling over theory.

## Key Concepts
- Short-lived access tokens and rotating refresh tokens
- Device fingerprint binding and PKCE
- Redis-backed jti store with fingerprint checks
- Cross-region revocation and token introspection
- Failure modes: token theft, replay, clock skew

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: issuing tokens
const access = signJWT({sub: userId, aud: 'api'}, {expiresIn: '15m'});
const refreshJti = uuid();
const fingerprint = getDeviceFingerprint(req);
storeRedis(`refresh:${refreshJti}`, {userId, fingerprint, exp: now+7*24*3600});
const refresh = signJWT({sub: userId, jti: refreshJti}, {expiresIn: '7d'});
```

